 for bestpractices and # AI Workspace Prompt for Development Tool

This prompt defines a comprehensive, end-to-end blueprint to build the AI Workspace with an integrated AI assistant/agent layer, prompt templates library, document data structure, and a global dashboard. It follows the required runtime safety rules (null/undefined guards, proper array handling, and useState defaults) and enforces dependency-first implementation order (auth/core data before dashboards/workspaces, when both are present).

- Target Page: AI Workspace
- Scope: Conversational AI interface scoped to tenant data with modes Ask, Analyze, Report, and Action. Includes conversation history and source citations.
- Core Components:
  - AI Assistant & Agent Layer
  - AI Prompt Templates Library
  - Indexed Documents for RAG (Drive/Notion/uploads)
  - Global Dashboard
- Data Model: Document (indexed), and related unified data context

Structure of this prompt is designed to be consumed by an AI development tool that will generate code, data models, API definitions, UI components, and acceptance tests.

1) AI Workspace Overview

- Objective: Build a tenant-scoped Conversational AI workspace with four modes (Ask, Analyze, Report, Action). The AI should retrieve tenant data, respect role-based access controls, and provide source citations for all responses. Conversation history must be stored and exportable. The system should trigger permitted actions with proper permission checks, and log AI-initiated actions.

- Constraints:
  - Multi-tenant isolation: tenantId must govern all data queries and AI context.
  - Role-based access: user role determines what AI can access and which actions are allowed.
  - Data provenance: all AI outputs that reference documents must include source citations and links back to the original source.
  - Safety: require explicit confirmation for sensitive actions; log AI-triggered actions.
  - Runtime safety: all code must guard against null/undefined values before using array methods; use data ?? [] and Array.isArray checks; initialize arrays with useState([]).

2) Page Description (Full Detail)

- What this page is:
  - The AI Workspace page is a chat-style interface tightly scoped to tenant data with four operational modes: Ask (Q&A with context), Analyze (data-driven analysis), Report (summaries/reports), and Action (trigger tasks/workflows).

- Goals:
  - Provide an intuitive chat interface that continuously retrieves tenant context, supports retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), uses tenant-specific prompt templates, surfaces source citations, and allows safe action triggering with permission checks.
  - Maintain conversation history with the ability to export/import transcripts.
  - Show a Context panel listing data sources and referenced documents; an Action Drawer surfaces AI-suggested actions with permission gating.

- Connected features:
  - AI Assistant & Agent Layer: role-aware AI with retrieval augmentation, prompt templates, source citations, and action triggering with per-user permission checks.
  - AI Prompt Templates Library: tenant-configurable prompts for executive summaries, pipeline analyses, project status, etc.
  - Document/Data Structure: indexed documents (Drive, Notion, uploads) used for retrieval (RAG) and context.
  - Global Dashboard: for navigation/summary context and role-based quick actions (accessible via the app’s global navigation).

- UI elements & guidance:
  - Chat panel: message list, composer, mode selector (Ask, Analyze, Report, Action) with keyboard-accessible controls; show role-appropriate action hints.
  - Conversation history: persistent timeline of messages with timestamps; export option (JSON/CSV/Transcript).
  - Context panel: shows data sources, document references, document metadata, source citations per response.
  - Action Drawer: AI-suggested actions with permission checks; allow preview/confirm before executing.
  - Source citations: inline linkable citations next to AI responses where possible (e.g., [Doc: Notion - Meeting Notes, 2024-09-01]).

- API integrations:
  - No external APIs are mandated in this design; however, plan for modular adapters for:
    - AI model provider (e.g., OpenAI, LlamaNext, or other vendor)
    - Document retrieval service (RAG embedding store)
    - Identity/Permissions service
    - Audit/logging service
  - All API results must be validated, and results should be guarded with the runtime-safety rules (null-safe access, array checks).

3) Components to Build

- AI Workspace UI Components
  - ModeSwitch: Tabbed switch with four modes; keyboard accessible; visually indicates current mode.
  - ConversationPanel: Chat list rendering, message grouping by user/AI, per-message citations.
  - MessageComposer: Text input, quick-reply templates, submit handler; support for streaming partial responses if using an async model.
  - ContextPanel: Tenant data sources, linked documents, metadata, and a quick filter by source/department.
  - ActionDrawer: Drawer containing AI-suggested actions with permission checks and confirmation step.
  - HistoryPanel: History listing with export controls; supports filtering by mode/period.
  - PromptTemplateEditor: Tenant-configurable prompt templates (for Ask/Analyze/Report/Action) with save/publish capability.
  - DocumentCard: Lightweight representation of a document with sourceProvider, externalId, and snippet; clickable to open the source.
  - KPIBar/Card: Lightweight metrics tiles showing session statistics, token usage, or activity counts.

- AI Assistant & Agent Layer
  - AIAssistantService
    - Inputs: tenantId, userId, userRole, workspaceContext (selected department/workspace), mode, user permissions, connected data sources.
    - Behavior: Retrieve relevant context; compose prompt using tenant templates; call model; parse response; attach citations; handle action triggers with permission checks.
    - Safeguards: Ensure any action payload is only allowed if user has permission; log action requests.
  - PromptTemplatesLibrary
    - Stores tenant-configurable prompts for: Executive Summary, Pipeline Analysis, Project Status, Team Performance, etc.
    - API to get templates by mode/department and to update templates per tenant.
  - RAGContextManager
    - Fetches and aggregates documents and structured data for retrieval augmented generation.
    - Uses guards for data absence: (data ?? []) and Array.isArray checks.
  - ActionEngine
    - Validate and execute permitted actions (create/update records, trigger workflows).
    - Requires confirmation step for sensitive actions.
  - SourceCitationRenderer
    - Renders inline citations in AI responses with document references and links to source.

- Asset: AI Prompt Templates Library
  - Tenant-configurable templates for:
    - Executive Summary
    - Pipeline Analysis
    - Project Status
    - Team Performance
    - Risk Summary
  - Storage: JSON-based template store with versioning and tenant scoping.
  - Validation: Template validation rules (non-empty prompt, placeholders, allowed tokens per mode).

- Asset: Document (Indexed Documents)
  - Schema (Data Structure)
    - id (uuid, required)
    - companyId (tenantId) (uuid, required)
    - sourceProvider (string, required) e.g., google_drive, notion
    - externalId (string, required)
    - text (text, required)
    - metadata (JSON)
    - createdAt (timestamp)
    - updatedAt (timestamp)
  - Relationships:
    - belongs_to Company (tenant)
  - Behavior:
    - Ingested from Drive/Notion/uploads
    - Indexed for search/RAG
    - Expose document snippet and full reference in citations
  - Safeguards:
    - Always guard with Array.isArray and null checks when mapping or reducing results
    - Normalize text to prevent extremely large payloads in prompts

- Page: Global Dashboard
  - High-level landing dashboard showing aggregated KPIs, alerts, recent activity, and quick actions by role
  - Widgets driven from unified data layer
  - Quick navigation to AI Workspace with contextual defaults

4) Implementation Requirements

Frontend
- Tech choices: React (with hooks), TypeScript, a UI framework consistent with design system (theming, tokens, components).
- Components and pages:
  - AIWorkspacePage: container that wires ModeSwitch, ConversationPanel, ContextPanel, ActionDrawer, HistoryPanel, and PromptTemplateEditor.
  - Use useState with typed arrays: e.g., const [messages, setMessages] = useState<Message[]>([]); const [documents, setDocuments] = useState<DocumentItem[]>([]);
  - Ensure all data arrays are initialized to [] and guarded with Array.isArray() before mapping/filtering.
  - All data fetched from APIs must coerce to safe shapes:
    - const list = Array.isArray(response?.data) ? response.data : [];
  - Implement null-safety throughout: optional chaining, defaults, and guards.
- UI behavior:
  - Mode switching triggers AIAssistantService with the selected mode
  - Conversation history export in JSON/CSV
  - Context panel shows sources, document references; citations appear in AI responses
  - Action Drawer shows permitted actions; on confirm, call ActionEngine
  - PromptTemplateEditor allows tenant to edit and save templates; preview mode shows generated prompts with placeholders populated

Backend
- Data layer:
  - Tables: tenants/companies (multi-tenant context), users, roles, permissions, documents (indexed), conversations, messages, templates, actions, audit logs, alerts, dashboards.
  - Document table shape:
    - id UUID PK
    - companyId UUID FK
    - sourceProvider VARCHAR
    - externalId VARCHAR
    - text TEXT
    - metadata JSONB
    - createdAt TIMESTAMP
    - updatedAt TIMESTAMP
  - Conversation / Message:
    - id UUID
    - tenantId UUID
    - userId UUID
    - mode ENUM(Ask, Analyze, Report, Action)
    - content TEXT
    - isUser BOOLEAN
    - createdAt TIMESTAMP
    - citations JSONB (array of { docId, snippet, source })
  - PromptTemplates:
    - id UUID
    - companyId UUID
    - mode ENUM
    - name VARCHAR
    - template TEXT
    - version INT
    - isActive BOOLEAN
    - createdAt TIMESTAMP
    - updatedAt TIMESTAMP
- AI/Agent Layer:
  - AIAssistantService endpoints:
    - POST /api/ai/workspace/:tenantId/:mode/message
      - Payload: { userId, workspaceContext, userRole, promptParameters, userInput }
      - Response: { reply, citations, actions: [...] }
  - ActionEngine endpoints:
    - POST /api/ai/workspace/:tenantId/actions/execute
      - Payload: { actionId, context, confirmation }
      - Behavior: validate permission, perform action (create/update/trigger), log activity
- Retrieval/Prompt assembly:
  - RAG: Retrieve relevant documents and data based on tenant context and user role
  - Build composite prompt using:
    - Tenant data context
    - Retrieved documents snippets
    - Selected PromptTemplate for mode
    - Action constraints and permission rules
- Security:
  - Auth: JWT/OAuth per project conventions; require authentication for all endpoints
  - Authorization: Role-based access checks; ensure actions are allowed per user role
  - Data leakage prevention: Ensure AI responses cannot reveal data the user is not allowed to access
  - Audit: Log AI-initiated actions; store actionId, userId, timestamp, result
- Validation:
  - Use server-side validation for all inputs
  - Normalize and sanitize outputs from the model before storing or returning
  - Validate document references in citations exist and are tenant-scoped

Integration
- How components connect:
  - Frontend calls AIAssistantService to generate a reply based on selected mode and user input
  - AIAssistantService uses RAG to fetch documents, then constructs a prompt via PromptTemplates
  - AIAssistantService returns reply with inline citations; UI renders citations via SourceCitationRenderer
  - If reply includes actions, the UI presents ActionDrawer with the actions; user confirmation triggers ActionEngine
  - PromptTemplateEditor updates tenant templates stored on the backend
  - Documents ingestion and indexing populate the Indexed Documents table and context referenced by AIAssistantService

5) User Experience Flow

- Step 1: User signs in (auth dependency completed first)
- Step 2: User lands on Global Dashboard; navigates to AI Workspace
- Step 3: User selects mode (Ask / Analyze / Report / Action)
- Step 4: User enters a message in the ConversationPanel
- Step 5: Frontend calls AIAssistantService with tenantId, userId, mode, and userMessage
- Step 6: Backend fetches tenant context, retrieves relevant documents (RAG), selects appropriate prompt template, runs model
- Step 7: AI returns reply with citations; UI renders messages and citations
- Step 8: If AI suggests actions, ActionDrawer opens with permitted actions; user confirms
- Step 9: On action confirmation, ActionEngine executes; logs outcome
- Step 10: Conversation history is saved; user can export
- Step 11: User can edit/adjust tenant PromptTemplates in PromptTemplateEditor; templates validate and save per tenant

6) Build Order & Dependencies (Mandatory)

- Prerequisites:
  - Authentication and user management module in place
  - Multi-tenant data model and tenant isolation implemented
  - Unified data/context layer and document ingestion pipeline scaffold
  - Role and permission model implemented
- Blocks:
  - AI Workspace UI requires AIAssistantService and RAG context to be available
  - Prompt Templates Library must be established to provision mode-specific prompts
  - Document ingest/index pipeline must be ready to supply indexed documents
- Sequencing Rule:
  - Auth/access control and core data setup must be completed before dashboard/workspace/analytics pages when both exist
  - Implement Global Dashboard first (as a landing container), then AI Workspace page
  - Then implement AI Assistant Layer with Prompt Templates, RAG, and Action Engine
  - Finally, implement UI polish, history export, and complete styling per design system

7) Technical Specifications

- Data Models
  - Document
    - id: UUID PK
    - companyId: UUID FK to Tenant
    - sourceProvider: string
    - externalId: string
    - text: string
    - metadata: JSONB
    - createdAt: timestamp
    - updatedAt: timestamp
  - Conversation / Message
    - id: UUID PK
    - tenantId: UUID
    - userId: UUID
    - mode: ENUM('Ask','Analyze','Report','Action')
    - content: string
    - isUser: boolean
    - createdAt: timestamp
    - citations: JSONB
  - PromptTemplate
    - id: UUID PK
    - tenantId: UUID
    - mode: ENUM('Ask','Analyze','Report','Action')
    - name: string
    - template: TEXT
    - version: INT
    - isActive: boolean
    - createdAt/updatedAt: timestamp
- API Endpoints
  - POST /api/ai/workspace/{tenantId}/{mode}/message
    - Body: { userId, workspaceContext, userRole, input }
    - Returns: { reply, citations, actions }
  - POST /api/ai/workspace/{tenantId}/actions/execute
    - Body: { actionId, context, confirmation }
    - Returns: { status, result }
  - GET /api/ai/workspace/{tenantId}/templates/{mode}
    - Returns: list of templates
  - PUT /api/ai/workspace/{tenantId}/templates/{mode}/{templateId}
    - Body: { template, version, isActive }
- Security
  - JWT-based authentication; authorization checks against role/tenant
  - Permissions wired to route guards
  - Sensitive actions require explicit confirmation
  - AI actions logged in an AuditLog
- Validation
  - Validate inputs server-side; use defaults where applicable
  - Ensure arrays are guarded: (data ?? []) or Array.isArray(data) ? data.map(...) : []
  - Destructure with defaults: const { items = [], count = 0 } = response ?? {}
  - Use optional chaining for nested properties: obj?.prop?.nested
- Validation Rules
  - Non-empty tenantId/userId/mode
  - Document citations must reference existing documents within tenancy
  - Action payloads validated against allowed actions per role
  - Model outputs sanitized and truncated as necessary for UI safety

8) Acceptance Criteria

- Functional:
  - AI Workspace loads per tenant with proper authentication
  - Mode switching updates prompt assembly and shows mode-specific behavior
  - AI responses include source citations with document references
  - Conversation history persists and exports correctly
  - Context panel lists tenant data sources and documents, filtered by mode/department
  - Action drawer shows only actions permitted by user role; executing actions requires confirmation
  - Prompt templates can be edited per tenant and saved; default templates exist
  - Data retrieval uses RAG and returns relevant context; no crash on empty results
- Performance:
  - Response latency under 1.5–2.5 seconds for typical context sizes (depending on model)
  - Caching of frequent prompts/templates per tenant to reduce repeated assembly time
- Security:
  - Access control enforced for all endpoints
  - No data leakage to unauthorized roles
  - All AI-triggered actions are logged
- Reliability:
  - Null/undefined guards prevent runtime errors when data is missing
  - All array operations guarded: (items ?? []).map(...) or Array.isArray(items) ? items.map(...) : []
- UI/UX:
  - Adheres to the design system with implemented typography, colors, spacing
  - Accessible controls (keyboard navigable, proper focus states)

9) UI/UX Guidelines (Applied Design System)

- Visual Style: Implement as described in the Visual Style section with color tokens and typography constraints
- Typography: Headings use Poppins/Manrope; body text uses Inter/Roboto; responsive scale for H1 (56–96px desktop)
- Spacing: 12-column responsive grid; hero left blocks 6–7 columns; right block 4–5 columns
- Card/Surface styling: deep surfaces, rounded corners, subtle inner shadows, faint borders
- Interaction: hover elevations, transitions 120–200ms, focus rings in pale blue
- Data Visualization: minimal flat visuals with specified color anchors
- Live indicators: neon green pulse for live status
- Accessibility: high-contrast components, aria-labels, and keyboard shortcuts where applicable

10) Visual Style Details (Summary)

- Color Palette and Typography: as provided in the project context (colors and font families)
- Card Design: deep surfaces (#0F1720), subtle borders (#154E78 at 6–8% opacity)
- Layout: 12-column grid; hero left/right balance
- Interactions: hover lift, subtle glow, focus rings
- Data Visualizations: pale sky blue and neon green for primary accents and live indicators
- UI language: enterprise-tech aesthetic with clarity and action-focus

11) Runtime Safety Rules (Mandatory)

- Supabase-like results: Use const items = data ?? [] for results
- Array methods: (items ?? []).map(...) or Array.isArray(items) ? items.map(...) : []
- useState for arrays: const [arr, setArr] = useState<Type[]>([])
- API response shapes: const list = Array.isArray(response?.data) ? response.data : []
- Optional chaining: use obj?.property?.nested when accessing nested API results
- Destructuring defaults: const { items = [], count = 0 } = response ?? {}
- Guard all potential null/undefined values before invoking array methods or rendering lists

12) Clustered Scope Items (if any)

The project context lists multiple major components as a cohesive unit (AI Workspace, AI Assistant Layer, Prompt Library, Document data, Global Dashboard). This prompt treats them as a single cohesive batch with clear subsections above. If you need a separate batch for each clustered item, the sections below can be expanded into separate deliverables with explicit acceptance criteria per item. For now, all related components are implemented in a unified batch to minimize duplication and ensure integrated behavior.

13) Deliverables Summary (What to Produce)

- Frontend:
  - AIWorkspacePage.tsx (with subcomponents: ModeSwitch, ConversationPanel, ContextPanel, ActionDrawer, HistoryPanel, PromptTemplateEditor)
  - AIWorkspace hooks/services for API calls
  - PromptTemplateEditor UI and tenant-config persistence
  - UI tests (component-level) ensuring correct rendering and mode behavior
- Backend:
  - API routes for workspace messages, actions, templates
  - Data models: Document, Conversation/Message, PromptTemplate
  - AIAssistantService logic with RAG integration point
  - ActionEngine with permission checks and audit logs
  - Validation and error handling
- Data:
  - Document schema and seed data
  - Prompt templates with default tenant-level templates
- Acceptance test suite:
  - Functional, performance, security checks as per criteria
  - End-to-end test for AI Workspace conversation, citations, and action triggering

If you’d like, I can tailor this prompt to a specific tech stack (e.g., Next.js + Zustand, or Remix + Prisma, or NestJS + Prisma) and include concrete file scaffolding, folder structure, and example code snippets that adhere to the runtime-safety rules above. for style.

CRITICAL DESIGN REQUIREMENTS:
- You MUST follow ALL design principles and patterns specified in the Design Reference above
- Do NOT deviate from the design specifications without explicit user permission
- Implement ALL visual design elements (colors, typography, spacing, shadows, animations) as specified
- Follow the layout patterns and component designs exactly as documented
- Ensure all interactions and micro-animations match the reference specifications

CRITICAL TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS:
- React 18.3.1, React Router 6.30.1
- Tailwind CSS v3 with tailwind.config.js and custom keyframes
- @tailwindcss/typography plugin for rich text content
- Tailwind CSS animations (NOT Motion library or framer-motion)
- Sonner for toasts
- Recharts for data visualization
- Native fetch() with API utilities in src/lib/api.ts
- Design system with CSS custom properties
- Use RGB color values for theming
- When Supabase is configured: use Edge Functions for LLM and server-only logic; never expose API keys in the client


=== EDGE FUNCTIONS & DATABASE MIGRATIONS (MANDATORY) ===

Even without a Supabase project connected, you MUST create the correct files when the task requires them:

EDGE FUNCTIONS - create supabase/functions/<function-name>/index.ts for:
- LLM/AI features (chat, completions, embeddings, vector search)
- Third-party API integrations (Stripe, SendGrid, Twilio, OpenAI, Anthropic, etc.)
- Incoming webhooks from external services
- Any server-only logic or logic that must not expose secrets to the client
- Any prompt that says "create edge function" or "add edge function"
File location: supabase/functions/<function-name>/index.ts
Import pattern: import { serve } from "https://deno.land/std@0.168.0/http/server.ts";
Never expose API keys or secrets in client-side code.

DATABASE MIGRATIONS - create supabase/migrations/<YYYYMMDDHHmmss>_<description>.sql for:
- Every new database table, view, or enum mentioned in the task
- Every schema change (ALTER TABLE, new index, new policy, etc.)
- Any prompt that says "create migration" or describes a data model
Make migrations idempotent (IF NOT EXISTS / CREATE OR REPLACE). Include RLS policies.
Do NOT run "supabase db push" or execute SQL directly — only create the .sql files.

MANDATORY CHECKLIST - Before finishing, verify:
- [ ] Migration SQL file(s) created for every database table/schema change mentioned
- [ ] Edge Function file(s) created for every server-side/secrets feature mentioned
- [ ] TypeScript types created for every new database table

